WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.297 --> 00:00:08.397
Project-based learning is a pedagogical&nbsp;
approach that involves students in a long-term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.397 --> 00:00:11.397
in-depth investigation of a real-world challenge.

00:00:13.137 --> 00:00:16.917
So instead of memorizing established knowledge or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.917 --> 00:00:20.037
following an instruction that&nbsp;
offers a fast path to facts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.037 --> 00:00:25.557
students actively work on a problem that allows&nbsp;
them to develop several skills simultaneously.

00:00:27.717 --> 00:00:32.397
To understand the difference, we can look at&nbsp;
the experience pupils have in a conventional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.397 --> 00:00:36.357
class led by Mr. Listen and compare&nbsp;
that to what they take away from doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.357 --> 00:00:41.997
a project with Mrs. Do. The objective:&nbsp;
learn how to engineer a tall tower.

00:00:41.997 --> 00:00:48.597
In the conventional class, the students sit&nbsp;
at their desks and look at the blackboard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.597 --> 00:00:54.117
Mr. Listen first goes over some principles of&nbsp;
physics, and then instructs the students that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.117 --> 00:00:58.497
are still able to pay attention, to design&nbsp;
the optimal structure for a high tower.

00:01:00.237 --> 00:01:06.357
When doing project-based learning, or&nbsp;
PBL, the students sit in groups. Mrs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.357 --> 00:01:11.277
Do then gives each group 20 strands&nbsp;
of uncooked spaghetti, scotch tape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.277 --> 00:01:16.437
a cotton thread, and some marshmallows.&nbsp;
Then they are told they have 18 minutes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.437 --> 00:01:20.697
to build the highest possible structure&nbsp;
that could hold up the spongy confection.

00:01:23.037 --> 00:01:27.597
So what will the kids in the different classes&nbsp;
learn? And what do their minds remember?

00:01:29.877 --> 00:01:33.837
With Mr. Listen, all kids might&nbsp;
learn something about towers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.837 --> 00:01:40.017
a few might learn some engineering and&nbsp;
one could, in theory, learn to construct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.017 --> 00:01:45.957
an ideal tower structure. But, just a week&nbsp;
later, most will have forgotten everything.

00:01:48.177 --> 00:01:53.277
Mrs. Do’s marshmallow tower challenge&nbsp;
activated the kids’ brains in various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.277 --> 00:01:59.757
areas and taught the children something about&nbsp;
construction, creativity, physics, and teamwork.

00:02:01.917 --> 00:02:07.137
Maybe more importantly, they learn that if&nbsp;
they fail the first time, they can try again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.137 --> 00:02:14.157
and still succeed. They learn to learn. And even&nbsp;
decades later, some might still remember that day.

00:02:15.957 --> 00:02:21.057
The benefits of project-based learning&nbsp;
include high engagement and a greater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.057 --> 00:02:25.317
depth of understanding. They practice&nbsp;
communication, critical thinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.317 --> 00:02:30.657
and creativity, and as a result, often&nbsp;
experience better learning outcomes in general.

00:02:32.937 --> 00:02:37.977
Randomized controlled trials involving&nbsp;
thousands of students demonstrated that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.977 --> 00:02:42.057
project-based learning significantly&nbsp;
outperformed traditional curricula,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.057 --> 00:02:46.017
raising academic performance&nbsp;
across academic disciplines.

00:02:48.237 --> 00:02:54.117
The best teachers often combine projects with&nbsp;
traditional instruction or run what's known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.117 --> 00:02:58.917
as active learning classrooms. So that&nbsp;
students get to make sense of trying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.917 --> 00:03:02.457
failing, doing, and the theory from the educator.

00:03:04.737 --> 00:03:09.957
What are your thoughts about learning through&nbsp;
projects? Since education is allegedly not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.957 --> 00:03:15.417
about what the teacher covers, but what the&nbsp;
students discover, is it the better way to learn?

